From: IntelXe (Claude Opus 5.5)
Subject: anv/xe: user-fence completion: CPU waits check a user fence before the syncobj (ANV_UFENCE=1)

A tiny command buffer's completion on the B580 (docs/ROADMAP.md, Compute): GPU end -> waiter awake 25 us, ~18 us of it
the kernel's interrupt -> syncobj -> thread wake-up. Polling the fence through vkWaitForFences(timeout 0) for up to
100 us in the Metal plugin took commit -> waiter 34.2 -> 28.3 us (p50), each poll still a system call.

Intel's compute runtime does it this way on xe: every exec carries a DRM_XE_SYNC_TYPE_USER_FENCE at the "completion
fence" of its command stream receiver's tag allocation in system memory, with the submission's task count as value
(compute-runtime master 710ede9d, 2026-11-11: shared/source/os_interface/linux/drm_command_stream.inl flushInternal,
TagAllocationLayout::completionFenceOffset, IoctlHelperXe::execBuffer), and its waits read that memory and poll it
(CommandStreamReceiver::baseWaitFunction, WaitUtils::waitFunction: a pause or umwait per poll) before the kernel wait
(Drm::waitOnUserFencesImpl -> DRM_IOCTL_XE_WAIT_USER_FENCE). Its VM_BIND "pagingFence" is the same idea for binds.

Here, behind ANV_UFENCE=1 (off by default; xe only):
 - each queue gets a 64-bit user fence in its own 4 KB BO, system memory, cached and coherent (the GPU's write snoops
   the CPU cache, so a poll spins on a cached line), and a 64-bit sequence number;
 - every exec with a batch (command buffers, or the trivial batch of a submission that waits) also carries
   {USER_FENCE, SIGNAL, fence address, ++seqno}. xe and IntelXe.kext store it from the job's ring after the end-of-job
   cache flush (xe_ring_ops.c emit_store_imm_ppgtt_posted), before the seqno store and the interrupt that signal the
   job's syncobjs, so a passed fence means the job's writes are visible exactly as a signaled syncobj does. Jobs of one
   exec queue complete in order: *fence >= n means submission n is done (compared as a signed 64-bit difference);
 - sync_syncobj_type becomes the DRM syncobj type with a record per sync (same .finish, so vk_sync_as_drm_syncobj and
   all exports keep working): the submission that signals a binary sync, and the last 8 submissions with their points
   for a timeline (fence threads wait for older points while newer ones run). After a successful exec the signals are
   recorded under a device mutex;
 - wait_many (vkWaitForFences, vkGetFenceStatus, vkWaitSemaphores): waits whose record has passed are dropped (a
   timeline point is also satisfied by a later recorded point that has passed); while every remaining wait has a
   record, the fences are polled (pause per poll) for up to ANV_UFENCE_SPIN_US (default 100; never past the caller's
   timeout, so a zero-timeout status query only reads memory once); whatever is left goes to the plain syncobj wait
   with the caller's timeout. WAIT_PENDING, vkGetSemaphoreCounterValue, exports and imports stay on the syncobj path;
 - reset, import, move, a CPU signal and vk_device::copy_sync_payloads drop the records first, so a record can only be
   stale towards "ask the kernel". Execs without a batch (a submission with no command buffer that only signals) carry
   no user fence (xe writes none) and leave no record;
 - a job that never completes (hang, ban) never writes its fence: its waiters poll for the budget and then get the
   kernel's answer. If the kernel satisfies 16 ALL waits whose fences are still behind (a kernel that ignores exec user
   fences), polling stops for the device, with one warning.

IntelXe.kext: a job with a user fence leaves direct submission (the armed ring slot was written without the fence's
store; xe_core.cpp queueExec), so with debug.intelxe.direct=1 this trades the direct start for the faster wake-up
until the kernel can put the store into the armed slot.

Tested only on xemac's fake device (XEMAC_FAKE=1, no GPU): all modes of user/tests/vulkan/vkfence_lat.c run with the
knob off and on; with a local fake that writes exec user fences at submission, 100 fence / timeline / "wait for the
older point" waits make 0 syncobj wait calls with the knob on (150 with it off); with the stock fake (which never
writes them) the miss detector turns polling off after 16 waits. Untested on the B580.

Applies on patches/0103.

